A||N||6||87ECO5||Best product to use to restore roll bars||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||05-10-2001||06:35 PM||||They're not really roll bars (I'm not sure what they're called) but they're round and black and run along the bottom edge of the doors on my Nissan Pathfinder 4X4.  You use them as a step to enter and exit the high vehicle.<P>My problem is they're beginning to rust and fade and I'd like to restore them <B>WITHOUT</B> having them powder-coated.  I'm under the assumption that powder-coating will be very expensive and this truck isn't high on my financial priority list.<P>My concern is that traditional paint won't withstand the abuse they'll get from winter salt and rocks thrown by the tires.<P>What can I use to bring these things back to life?  The truck also has one of those brush guards on the front that's in the same condition.  Like the step bars, I'm concerned that paint on the brush guard won't stand up to bugs and rocks and the like.<P>Any help is greatly appreciated.<P>TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||05-10-2001||08:34 PM||||Scott--They're called nerf bars and my suggestion would be to have them sprayed with one of the pickup truck bedliners such as Rhinoliner.  It comes in different colors (not just generic black) and is rubbery and textured for a good grip (don't want to slip off).  I don't know about any but the Rhinoliner, but I had a pickup bed sprayed with that a few years ago.  Dynamite couldn't tear it up.  I wouldn't imagine it would cost much, either.  You'd probably need to remove them and sand them clean 1st.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-10-2001||08:54 PM||||I like that idea. Bed liner should work good. Done right that stuff is extremely tough.<P>Check your local stores. Some brands of bed liner are available off the shelf now. Including a couple of the "good" ones.||||reg||1
Z||000003||87ECO5||05-10-2001||09:59 PM||||Hmmmm ... I like that idea!  Thanks, guys!<P>Ogre - can you give me some examples of those "good" ones?||||reg||14
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||05-10-2001||10:30 PM||||Also try East Wood. They have lots of different paints and coatings!<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||4
Z||000005||theogre||05-10-2001||11:35 PM||||I forget brands right now... other things to worry about....<P>a couple brands you used to have be "profesionally done" now have kits on the shelf in many parts stores.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Firestarter_87||05-11-2001||02:15 PM||||My Eastwood catalog offers Gator Guard II Epoxy Bed Liner.  The kit includes two cans, one of part A and one of part B.  They cover 32 ft^2 at 5 mil THK.  Most trucks require 2 kits.  1 kit = $44.99<P>Firestarter||||reg||1
